<article class="bm-post">

  <header class="bm-col">
    <p class="bm-eyebrow">Physics Factory · Cadgen2 · motorsport aerodynamics</p>
    <h1>Real-Time Modification of <span class="bm-o">F1 Aerodynamic Surfaces</span></h1>
    <p class="bm-standfirst">Cadgen2 creates engineering-grade assemblies directly from specifications, generates precise design variations featuring defeatured geometry, BOM and STEP outputs, and extracts DOE parameterisations straight from imported CAD models. This article covers the second case: a 2022-generation Formula 1 car, where the parameterisation was derived from the supplied surface with no feature tree, and the front wing, wheel arch and rear diffuser are modified simultaneously and interactively.</p>
    <p class="bm-meta">BeyondMath · September 2026</p>
  </header>

  <figure class="bm-wide">
    <img src="assets/f1-live-doe-poster.jpg" alt="Cadgen2 modifying an F1 front wing: flap angle handle at -3.84 degrees, with displacement shown as a colour field over the wing surface" width="1920" height="1103">
    <figcaption>A single change at work: flap angle on elements 3 and 4, swept within its −4 … 4° DOE range. Colour is displacement as a fraction of that part's own largest move; the rest of the car is ghosted.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- ── The aerodynamic problem ── -->
  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>The front wing sets the flow structure for everything behind it. Its wake conditions the flow onto the floor leading edge, the sidepod inlets, the brake ducts and ultimately the diffuser, so a change at the wing propagates the length of the car. The wheel arch operates directly in that wake and in the tyre's separated flow, and the diffuser works on whatever reaches it. Performance differences between candidate designs come from a small set of geometric quantities:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Front wing:</strong> element angles, the slot gaps between elements, spanwise twist distribution, and endplate geometry, which governs how much flow is turned outboard of the front tyre.</li>
      <li><strong>Wheel arch:</strong> cowl height and incidence, fence dish, and the upper and lower slot gaps and shifts that bleed air through the arch rather than around it.</li>
      <li><strong>Rear diffuser:</strong> ramp angle, roof height at the throat and at the exit, keel height, tunnel width and roof camber, plus the incidence, camber and twist of the strakes standing inside each tunnel.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Establishing which combination performs requires generating and evaluating many variants of these surfaces. Doing that in conventional CAD is slow and expensive, for reasons specific to this class of geometry:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>There is no parametric controls to drive.</strong> Aerodynamic surfaces are crafted meshes or sewn B-rep, produced by surfacing and CFD iteration. No feature tree exists, so a dimensional change is not a parameter edit, it is a re-surfacing operation.</li>
      <li><strong>Edits must preserve continuity and interfaces.</strong> A modified element has to retain tangency and curvature continuity with its neighbours, keep its mounting faces where the structure expects them, and stay inside its regulatory volume. Each of those is a manual check.</li>
      <li><strong>Parts are coupled.</strong> Raising a diffuser tunnel roof moves the strakes standing on it. Changing the wing must not move its mount. Propagating a change correctly across an assembly multiplies both the work and the opportunities for error.</li>
      <li><strong>The parameter count defeats manual iteration.</strong> Roughly thirty geometric quantities span these three assemblies. At five levels of each that is a combinatorial space in the millions, and a useful subset is still thousands of variants. At hours of surfacing work per variant, the study is not reachable by hand.</li>
      <li><strong>Every variant needs a validity check before it can be solved.</strong> A morph or a re-surface that folds the mesh, inverts elements or creases the surface will fail in the mesher or, worse, produce a plausible but wrong CFD result.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>The constraint</h2>
    <p>Producing engineering-grade CAD remains a manual operation. Three costs dominate:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Synchronisation across a multipart assembly.</strong> A dimensional change propagates to mating parts, clearances, fastener positions and the specification documents. Each propagation path is a place where the model and the drawing can diverge.</li>
      <li><strong>Constraint compliance.</strong> Wall thicknesses, clearances, draft, minimum feature sizes and material allowables are enforced by review rather than by construction, so violations are found late.</li>
      <li><strong>No physical evaluation in the design environment.</strong> CAD systems do not report physical performance or cost during modelling. Assessing these requires exporting to a separate simulation tool, meshing, solving and interpreting. This loop is measured in days per iteration, which limits most programmes to a handful of candidate geometries.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Cadgen2 is an AI-driven CAD engine built to remove those three costs. It automates creation, modification and specification of assemblies, and connects directly to physics-based evaluation so that variants can be generated and assessed without leaving the workflow. Output is manufacturing-ready: per-part specifications, GD&amp;T records, a bill of materials and STEP geometry.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Capabilities</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automated design creation.</strong> Complete assemblies generated from a written specification, a sketch, a reference image or a parameter table.</li>
      <li><strong>Precision design variations.</strong> Model variants driven from natural-language instructions against a fully attributed component specification, so a change can be scoped to one dimension on one part or applied across an entire assembly.</li>
      <li><strong>Simulation defeaturing.</strong> Geometry prepared for meshing as part of generation rather than as a downstream cleanup pass, with the features that carry load retained and the features that only add elements removed.</li>
      <li><strong>Physics AI training data generation.</strong> Geometry variations generated across the design space, simulated and prepared for training Physics AI models.</li>
      <li><strong>End-to-end workflows.</strong> Integrates seamlessly with Physics Factory to transition from initial design space concepts to consolidated datasets, enabling physics AI model training and direct feedback into iterative design workflows.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Generated models are checked rather than assumed correct. Non-physical geometry, specification inconsistencies and constraint violations are detected and resolved during generation, and the checks run on the exported files rather than on the in-memory model.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="bm-rule bm-col">

  <!-- ── The Cadgen2 approach ── -->
  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>The Cadgen2 approach</h2>
    <p>Inputs to the study were the geometry, the permitted dimensional ranges and the optimisation objectives. No parametric model, feature tree or construction history was supplied, and no handles were defined by hand.</p>
    <p>Cadgen2 analysed the supplied CAD directly to determine the parameterisation, such as which quantities are available as control handles, where each handle's region of influence begins and ends, and how the displacement blends into the surrounding surface. It produced <strong>28 DOE handles</strong> across three assemblies:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Front wing — 8 handles.</strong> Flap angle on elements 3 and 4 together, top flap adjuster on element 4 alone, flap twist across span, mainplane angle on elements 1 and 2, the E2–E3 slot gap, endplate kick and endplate lean.</li>
      <li><strong>Wheel arch — 8 handles.</strong> Spanwise arch, cowl height, cowl incidence, fence dish, upper and lower slot gap, upper and lower slot shift.</li>
      <li><strong>Rear diffuser — 7 handles.</strong> Ramp angle, roof at the throat, roof at the exit, central keel height, tunnel roof camber, outer wall and tunnel width, roof twist about the tunnel.</li>
      <li><strong>Diffuser strakes, two per tunnel — 5 handles.</strong> Incidence, camber, twist root to tip, tip curl, lower edge at the exit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Three properties of that parameterisation matter more than the handle count.</p>
    <p><strong>Modification is multi-assembly and simultaneous.</strong> A handle acts on a collection of surfaces, not one patch. Flap angle rotates elements 3 and 4 together because that is the aerodynamically meaningful degree of freedom. Both sides of the car morph, with the right derived as the mirror of the left. In the study the car is drawn as 46 parts, of which the front wing and the wheel arch morph while the remainder holds as built.</p>
    <p><strong>Engineering interfaces are held automatically.</strong> The front wing mount band, |y| ≤ 170 mm, carries zero weight for every handle, so the wing continues to meet its mounting face exactly at every DOE setting rather than being pulled off it by a nearby deformation. The wheel arch in this study is an imported surface, and it occupies the same space as the parametric arch it replaces — spatial alignment is maintained implicitly rather than being re-established after each edit.</p>
    <p><strong>Displacement fields are defined over space, not over parts.</strong> A diffuser handle that raises the tunnel roof also moves the strakes standing on it, because the field is evaluated at their positions. The strakes ride the roof rather than detaching from it, which is the behaviour the physical assembly has.</p>
  </section>

  <figure class="bm-wide">
    <video class="bm-video" id="bm-f1-video" loop muted playsinline controls preload="metadata" poster="assets/f1-live-doe-poster.jpg">
      <source src="assets/f1-live-doe.mp4" type="video/mp4">
    </video>
    <figcaption>The full walkthrough. Each of the 28 handles is swept to the upper limit of its DOE range; colour shows how far each point moves as a fraction of that handle's own largest displacement, and per-handle validity metrics are reported on the frame throughout.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <!-- ── Validity ── -->
  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Validity is measured, not assumed</h2>
    <p>Every handle is swept to its DOE limit to confirm the morph remains valid across the complete range, and the result is measured on the mesh rather than inspected by eye. Representative readings at the limit:</p>
    <div class="bm-tablewrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Handle validity at the DOE limit · measured on the mesh</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Handle</th><th>Range</th><th>Material moved</th><th>Crease p99</th><th>Surface creased</th><th>Folds</th><th>Flipped area</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>flap angle (E3+E4)</td><td>−4 … 4°</td><td>20.45 mm</td><td>2.6°</td><td>0%</td><td>0</td><td>0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>upper slot gap (arch)</td><td>−4 … 8 mm</td><td>8 mm</td><td>15.2°</td><td>4.02%</td><td>0</td><td>0.0009%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>roof at the throat</td><td>−15 … 15 mm</td><td>15 mm</td><td>6.2°</td><td>0.62%</td><td>0</td><td>0%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The quantities are the ones that determine whether a surface can be meshed and solved. <em>Crease</em> is the added dihedral angle between adjacent facets, reported at the 99th percentile together with the fraction of surface area affected. <em>Folds</em> counts self-intersections of the deformed surface. <em>Flipped area</em> is the proportion of the surface whose normal has inverted. Across the three handles above, and across the sweep as a whole, folds are zero and flipped area is zero or 0.0009%, with vertex ordering preserved to a maximum of 0.009 mm. Morphed surfaces pass to a mesher without a repair step.</p>
    <p>Two limits are worth stating. The displacement field deforms a supplied baseline: it does not add or remove topology, so a feature absent from the import cannot be introduced by a handle. And on a B-rep the field is fitted to the NURBS poles rather than applied exactly, with the residual measured against the exact field and the fit rejected if it exceeds tolerance in the moved region; on a mesh the vertices move exactly.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ── What it changes ── -->
  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>What real-time modification changes</h2>
    <p>A single handle applies to the full surface in well under a second. On a comparable 90,497-triangle car surface, one handle applies in 465–547 ms measured across three settings, which places modification inside an interactive loop rather than a rebuild cycle. Three consequences follow:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A DOE becomes a table rather than a modelling programme.</strong> Each variant is a set of handle values. Generating the geometry for several hundred points is a scripted sweep, and each point is reproducible from its parameter values rather than stored as a separate file.</li>
      <li><strong>Exploration and evaluation converge.</strong> Because the variants are valid on generation, they can be passed to a solver, or to a trained physics AI model, without a manual repair stage between design and assessment.</li>
      <li><strong>The parameterisation is reusable.</strong> Handles derived from the geometry apply to any surface of the same topology, so a new baseline inherits the study rather than requiring it to be rebuilt.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The first case in this series is covered in <a target="_blank" href="../20260919-cadgen2-tablet-variant-study/">the companion post</a>: an engineering assembly generated from a written specification, expanded to 50 variants and taken through explicit structural simulation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="bm-col">
    <h2>Fully AI-driven CAD design and optimisation</h2>
    <p>Cadgen2 is a component of the Physics Factory platform, and it addresses the early-stage constraint directly: generating the geometric variation that physics-based optimisation requires. Those variants train models built on BeyondMath's foundational Physics AI, and the trained model then allows a design to be optimised against the underlying physics rather than against a proxy.</p>
    <p>A design team exploring a released product needs a handful of controlled variants with correct specifications and a BOM. A training campaign needs several hundred decorrelated variants with solver decks attached. Both are a parameter table driven through one model, and in both the variant is recorded as a state rather than as a file and reproducible from a few hundred bytes, and recomputed identically on every rebuild.</p>
  </section>

